  "learningBlocks": [
    {
      "id": "l13-b1",
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Future forms move the clause forward",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The future presents an action or state as not yet complete. Time words such as semper, donec and hora help orient the clause.",
        "fr": "Le futur présente une action ou un état comme non encore accompli. Des mots comme semper, donec et hora aident à situer la phrase dans le temps."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Les formes du futur font avancer la proposition"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l13-b2",
      "type": "verb-system",
      "title": "erit anchors the future of sum",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The future of sum is ero, eris, erit, erimus, eritis, erunt. In Cuius regni non erit finis, erit gives the future state.",
        "fr": "Le futur de sum est ero, eris, erit, erimus, eritis, erunt. Dans Cuius regni non erit finis, erit exprime l'état futur."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "erit sert de repère pour le futur de sum"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l13-b3",
      "type": "morphology",
      "title": "Two major future patterns",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "First/second-conjugation verbs commonly show -bo/-bis/-bit; third/fourth conjugations commonly show -am/-es/-et. Read the ending together with the verb family.",
        "fr": "Les verbes des première/deuxième conjugaisons montrent souvent -bo/-bis/-bit ; ceux des troisième/quatrième, -am/-es/-et. Lisez la terminaison avec la famille verbale."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Deux grands schémas du futur"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l13-b4",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Cuius regni non erit finis",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-credo-finis-l13",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Once erit is recognized, the structure is clear: there will be no end. finis is new; cuius remains a later relative-pronoun form.",
        "fr": "Dès que erit est reconnu, la structure devient claire : il n'y aura pas de fin. finis est nouveau ; cuius reste une forme relative étudiée plus tard."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-credo-finis-l13",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Cuius regni non erit finis"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l13-b5",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Introibo",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-introibo-l13",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Introibo is future: 'I will go in'. The phrase was already familiar; Lesson 13 makes its time value explicit.",
        "fr": "Introibo est au futur : « j'irai ». La formule était déjà familière ; la leçon 13 rend explicite sa valeur temporelle."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-introibo-l13",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Introibo"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l13-b6",
      "type": "time-words",
      "title": "semper, donec, hora",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "semper marks continuity, donec sets a temporal boundary, and hora locates an event in time. These become essential narrative scaffolding. The Creed's et iterum venturus est introduces iterum ('again'), a true tracked temporal adverb, not merely a gloss.",
        "fr": "semper marque la continuité, donec fixe une limite temporelle, et hora situe un événement dans le temps. Ce sont des repères essentiels du récit. Dans le Credo, et iterum venturus est introduit iterum (« de nouveau »), adverbe temporel désormais suivi."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "semper, donec, hora"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
